  colA.b41.s109 -> st1.h0.s112: {class: hop}
  st1.h40.s152 -> st2.h0.s153: {class: hop; label: "; > CREATE"}
  st1.h5.s117 -> colA.w43.s111: {class: call; label: "call"}
  st2.h5.s158 -> colA.w43.s111: {class: call}
  endcol: "the end of R2 and what the first correct long run sets off" {
    class: col; grid-columns: 1; grid-gap: 14
    b40: "40 · build" {class: neuron; s250: "10 > - | 60 out | 48 memory"; tooltip: "The build neuron: ’the last build word was the build neuron... routing information for the mother... build neuron = routing to sql prompt’ (10-26 03:50). Its DNA row, picked by before (10 ;) and remainder (none), is a build row ’because of how the before and remainder are set’, not by counting BUILDs. No remainder: this shard ends R2, so it carries R2’s memory node. The build neuron ends every born run, so it has one memory node per born run (his words 10-28)."}
    text: "the product of the run: the two statements as text, hole filled from cell value = 61" {shape: page; tooltip: "10-19 23:03: the statement ’as the product of the run’. 10-24 msg 36: the run data sheet ’then produces the exact code that sql needs to run as output the same way that it forms human sentences... that output is given to the mother to route to the sql prompt’."}
    reply: "SQL answers: ok" {class: sheet; tooltip: "The answer comes back through the mother > encoder mailbox as secondary input and fills R2’s cell reply (10-24 msg 36; 10-26 03:50). An error is not an end: ’the brain would problem solve’ (10-27 03:52)."}
    m2: "memory node on 40.s250  (R2)" {class: mem; run: "R2"; input: "receipt: 41.s109 4.s112 ... 10.s249 40.s250"; source: "R1 (no gate at 61)"; c_value: "61"; c_text: "> the product"; c_reply: "ok"; c_exists: "1 (read run 46)"; c_formula: "> receipt with the hole at 18.s117"; c_backpop: "1"; tooltip: "R2’s memory row on the shard that ended it, with its data sheet inside (10-26 03:50; his v4 run_sheet). Cells declared by the triggers that tripped: value (write run), text and reply (build), exists (read run), formula (decide run), backpop (write run)."}
    tl2: "timeline row: t1 | R2 > 40.s250 | 41 61" {class: tl; tooltip: "One row per run that ends: the clock reading, the receipt pointer, a couple of key words (10-16 06:40)."}
    b40.s250 -> text
    text -> reply: {class: route; label: "60 out > mother > neuron prompt > SQL > mother > encoder"}
    reply -> m2: {class: fill; label: "fills reply"}
    m2 -> tl2: {class: trig; label: "memory > timeline, stops"}
    r46: "46 · read" {class: neuron; s251: "40 > - | 60 out | -"; tooltip: "[reading] ’the first time it works, and is correct’ (his words 10-28): ok says it ran; correct is read back: the read run asks the prompt (.schema of 61) through mother and the answer fills cell exists = 1. Which check counts as ’correct’ is his call."}
    d53: "53 · decide (formula)" {class: neuron; s252: "46 > 43 | 44 copy | -"; tooltip: "His words 10-28: ’the first time it works, and is correct, it triggers a decide formula run that decides what the formula is, then writes it in that runs data sheet’. The decide formula run reads R2’s receipt, marks the hole (the value neuron’s shard 18.s117), and writes the formula into R2’s sheet cell formula: the receipt with the hole. The formula is a pointer to the run, never text (10-23 08:47: ’the row holds pointers to the run through the neurons that makes that’)."}
    f55: "55 · formula" {class: neuron; s253: "4 > 40 | 43 write, 54 substitute | -"; tooltip: "The formula neuron for CREATE TABLE value ( shard INTEGER , before INTEGER , trigger INTEGER , call INTEGER , spawn INTEGER ) (his words 10-28). Its new row s253: arrived from 4 (CREATE arriving from born), goes on to 40 (build); calls the write run (43) and the substitution run (54). The row’s trigger points at R2’s sheet cell formula (the receipt with the hole). [open] one formula neuron with a row per formula, as drawn, or one neuron per formula (10-23 08:47 ’a pointer to the shortcut neuron formula’)."}
    bp: "back population: 4.s112 gets  jump > 55.s253" {class: note; tooltip: "His words 10-28: ’through back population the triggers or calls or jumps or hops from a run create table (value) would have to show the substitution run and the write run writing the value... so that you can then skip the long run and use the formula neuron’. The write run (43) updates the first shard of the long run (CREATE arriving from born) with a jump to the formula row. 10-23 P5-9: back population is the application of a newly understood process to the other things it fits."}
    m2 -> r46.s251: {class: trig; label: "reply ok"}
    r46.s251 -> d53.s252: {class: trig; label: "exists = 1: first time correct"}
    d53.s252 -> m2: {class: fill; label: "writes formula"}
    d53.s252 -> f55.s253: {class: trig; label: "write run lays the formula row"}
    f55.s253 -> bp: {class: trig}
  }
  st2.h96.s249 -> endcol.b40.s250: {class: hop}
  colA.w43.s111 -> endcol.m2: {class: fill; label: "value = 61"}
  endcol.bp -> st1.h0: {class: jump; label: "jump laid on 4.s112"}
}
main.hall.ha -> born61.colA.b41: {class: trig; label: "no gate: born"}
born61.endcol.m2 -> main.hall.ha: {class: fill; label: "gate.61 = 61; try the run again [open: inline or retry]"}
born_short: "5 · BORN RUNS R4 (62) and R6 (63), THE SHORTCUT: born > CREATE jumps to the formula row; write run writes the value; substitution run plugs it in; build (his words 10-28)" {
  class: lane
  grid-columns: 2
  R4: "R4: born 62" {
    class: col; grid-columns: 1; grid-gap: 14
    b41: "41 · born" {class: neuron; s255: "62 > 4 | - | 51 ask"; tooltip: "The same born neuron, a new shard for this run: arrived from 62, goes on to 4 (CREATE); spawns the ask run (51) for the newborn's sources."}
    c4: "4 · CREATE" {class: hit; s112: "41 > 3 | - | -   jump > 55.s253"; tooltip: "The same shard 4.s112 the long run wrote, now carrying the jump laid by back population: the long run is skipped. 10-08: ’if I know where I am standing I can hop to any point in my brain in one step... all the work was done at learning, not at runtime’."}
    f55: "55 · formula" {class: neuron; s253: "4 > 40 | 43 write, 54 substitute | -"; tooltip: "The formula row laid after R2. Calls the write run and the substitution run; goes on to build."}
    w43: "43 · write" {class: neuron; s258: "55 > - | - | -"; tooltip: "The write run writes the value variable, 62, into R4's sheet cell value (his words 10-28: 'the formula shortcut is just to the data sheet writing the value varible')."}
    sub54: "54 · substitute" {class: neuron; s259: "55 > - | 44 copy | -"; tooltip: "The substitution run: ’substitution run grabbing that value and plugging it into the run data sheet so a new memory node can be made or a new neuron can be made’ (his words 10-28). Its call: the copy run (44) copies the formula (R2’s sheet cell formula, the receipt with the hole) into this run’s sheet; then the hole is filled with cell value = 62. ’the trigger of the substitution run copying into the substitution run’ (10-28)."}
    cp44: "44 · copy" {class: neuron; s260: "54 > - | - | -"; tooltip: "Copies R2.sheet.formula into R4's sheet cell text (the receipt with the hole)."}
    b40: "40 · build" {class: neuron; s261: "55 > - | 60 out | 48 memory"; tooltip: "The build neuron again, a new shard for R4: it ends the run, so it carries another memory node. The build neuron now holds memory nodes for R2, R4, ... one per born run."}
    m: "memory node on 40.s261  (R4)" {class: mem; run: "R4"; input: "receipt: 41.s255 4.s112 55.s253 40.s261"; source: "R1 (no gate at 62)"; c_value: "62"; c_text: "> R2.formula with 62 in the hole"; c_reply: "ok"; tooltip: "A separate memory node on a separate ending shard of the same neuron 40, with its own sheet inside (his words 10-28)."}
    tl: "timeline row: t | R4 > 40.s261 | 41 62" {class: tl}
    b41.s255 -> c4.s112: {class: hop}
    c4.s112 -> f55.s253: {class: jump; label: "jump"}
    f55.s253 -> w43.s258: {class: call}
    f55.s253 -> sub54.s259: {class: call}
    sub54.s259 -> cp44.s260: {class: call}
    w43.s258 -> m: {class: fill; label: "value = 62"}
    cp44.s260 -> m: {class: fill; label: "text"}
    f55.s253 -> b40.s261: {class: hop}
    b40.s261 -> m: {class: route; label: "60 out > mother > prompt > ok > encoder > reply"}
    m -> tl: {class: trig}
  }
  R6: "R6: born 63" {
    class: col; grid-columns: 1; grid-gap: 14
    b41: "41 · born" {class: neuron; s262: "63 > 4 | - | 51 ask"; tooltip: "The same born neuron, a new shard for this run: arrived from 63, goes on to 4 (CREATE); spawns the ask run (51) for the newborn's sources."}
    c4: "4 · CREATE" {class: hit; s112: "41 > 3 | - | -   jump > 55.s253"; tooltip: "The same shard 4.s112 the long run wrote, now carrying the jump laid by back population: the long run is skipped. 10-08: ’if I know where I am standing I can hop to any point in my brain in one step... all the work was done at learning, not at runtime’."}
    f55: "55 · formula" {class: neuron; s253: "4 > 40 | 43 write, 54 substitute | -"; tooltip: "The formula row laid after R2. Calls the write run and the substitution run; goes on to build."}
    w43: "43 · write" {class: neuron; s265: "55 > - | - | -"; tooltip: "The write run writes the value variable, 63, into R6's sheet cell value (his words 10-28: 'the formula shortcut is just to the data sheet writing the value varible')."}
    sub54: "54 · substitute" {class: neuron; s266: "55 > - | 44 copy | -"; tooltip: "The substitution run: ’substitution run grabbing that value and plugging it into the run data sheet so a new memory node can be made or a new neuron can be made’ (his words 10-28). Its call: the copy run (44) copies the formula (R2’s sheet cell formula, the receipt with the hole) into this run’s sheet; then the hole is filled with cell value = 63. ’the trigger of the substitution run copying into the substitution run’ (10-28)."}
    cp44: "44 · copy" {class: neuron; s267: "54 > - | - | -"; tooltip: "Copies R2.sheet.formula into R6's sheet cell text (the receipt with the hole)."}
    b40: "40 · build" {class: neuron; s268: "55 > - | 60 out | 48 memory"; tooltip: "The build neuron again, a new shard for R6: it ends the run, so it carries another memory node. The build neuron now holds memory nodes for R2, R6, ... one per born run."}
    m: "memory node on 40.s268  (R6)" {class: mem; run: "R6"; input: "receipt: 41.s262 4.s112 55.s253 40.s268"; source: "R1 (no gate at 63)"; c_value: "63"; c_text: "> R2.formula with 63 in the hole"; c_reply: "ok"; tooltip: "A separate memory node on a separate ending shard of the same neuron 40, with its own sheet inside (his words 10-28)."}
    tl: "timeline row: t | R6 > 40.s268 | 41 63" {class: tl}
    b41.s262 -> c4.s112: {class: hop}
    c4.s112 -> f55.s253: {class: jump; label: "jump"}
    f55.s253 -> w43.s265: {class: call}
    f55.s253 -> sub54.s266: {class: call}
    sub54.s266 -> cp44.s267: {class: call}
    w43.s265 -> m: {class: fill; label: "value = 63"}
    cp44.s267 -> m: {class: fill; label: "text"}
    f55.s253 -> b40.s268: {class: hop}
    b40.s268 -> m: {class: route; label: "60 out > mother > prompt > ok > encoder > reply"}
    m -> tl: {class: trig}
  }
}
main.hall.hc -> born_short.R4.b41: {class: trig; label: "no gate: born"}
main.hall.he -> born_short.R6.b41: {class: trig; label: "no gate: born"}
born_short.R4.m -> main.hall.hc: {class: fill; label: "gate.62 = 62"}
born_short.R6.m -> main.hall.he: {class: fill; label: "gate.63 = 63"}
sources: "6 · SOURCE RUNS R3 (61), R5 (62), R7 (63): the newborn's first memory is its definition, pulled from every source and compared (10-23 P5-9; 10-25 msg 51). Each source run has its own sheet; one answer goes back to R1." {
  class: lane
  grid-columns: 3
  src61: "source run for 61 · define" {
    class: col; grid-columns: 1; grid-gap: 14
    a51: "51 · ask" {class: neuron; s269: "41 > 60 | - | -"; tooltip: "The ask run, spawned by the born run. Declares its own sheet: one cell per source (wn, gcide, mw, mwt, moby, map, sqldoc) and compile; declares on R1’s sheet the cell def.61. Assembles the ask from the encoder’s label of the newborn and goes on to 60 (out): the brain > mother mailbox. The run names the sources; mother carries the ask to each one’s inbox and decides nothing (10-23 P2; 10-23 01:28 asking the sources is the brain’s decide run)."}
    sh: "sheet of the source run (define)" {class: sheet; c_wn: "1 line(s)" ; c_gcide: "1 line(s)" ; c_mw: "2 line(s)" ; c_mwt: "4 line(s)" ; c_moby: "3 line(s)" ; c_map: "1 line(s)" ; c_sqldoc: "0: empty"; c_compile: "> definition node of 61"; tooltip: "The source run's own sheet: a cell per source, filled by each return; when no declared cell is empty the compile runs (10-26 03:50). Counts are the real lines in the archived slice (tries/11 sat172.json)."}
    a51.s269 -> sh: {class: readw}
