born_short.R6.b41 -> sources.src63.a51: {class: spawn}
after: "7 · THE THREE NEWBORNS AFTER THE LINE: DNA, definition node with its rows and relationships, memory nodes per ending shard" {
  class: lane
  grid-columns: 3
  n61: "61 · define" {
    class: col; grid-columns: 1; grid-gap: 14
    dna: "61 · define  DNA" {class: newborn; s104: "1 > 3 | - | -"; tooltip: "One row so far: the one R1 wrote. No trigger yet: define has no action until a formula is taught or learned for it (10-25 msg 51: define : dog = look up definition memory, print)."}
    defn: "definition node of 61" {class: defn; def1: "wn: > return run (…determine the essential qual…)"; def2: "gcide: > return run (…To fix the bounds of; to bri…)"; def3: "mw: > return run (…to determine or identify the…)"; def4: "mw: > return run (…to discover and set forth th…)"; rel5: "like > 71 (delineate)"; rel6: "like > 99 (outline)"; rel7: "like > 100 (silhouette)"; rel8: "like > 101 (sketch)"; rel9: "like > 102 (assign)"; rel10: "like > 103 (baptize)"; rel11: "like > 163 (be characteristic)"; rel12: "like > 106 (explain)"; tooltip: "Rows laid by the compile from the real source lines of the archived slice: wn 1, gcide 1, mw 2 definitions; mwt 4, moby 3, map 1 'like' lines, which are relationship rows: like > the gate of the related symbol (born as they arrive). 10-23 P4: 'any section that can have a relationship to something else is a self-fulfilling relationship section. All of them.'"}
    mem_note: "no memory node yet: no run has ended at 61" {class: note; tooltip: "Memory nodes exist only on shards that end runs (his words 10-28; 10-23 P2 B2)."}
    dna -> defn: {class: readw; label: "born with it"}
  }
  n62: "62 · :" {
    class: col; grid-columns: 1; grid-gap: 14
    dna: "62 · :  DNA" {class: newborn; s106: "3 > 3 | - | -"; s_ret: "68 > 69 | - | -   (from the mw return run)"; tooltip: "Two rows after this line: R1's (space > space) and the mw return run's (that > whoever). Two arrivals, two rows: the key is before and remainder (§12 law 13). When two rows share one arrival, the call column holds the inspection run (lane 8)."}
    defn: "definition node of 62" {class: defn; empty: "no rows: no source holds :"; sqldoc: "(the SQL docs would hold its action in code: the hole mark)"; tooltip: "No source in the archived slice answers for ':'. The cells stay 0 and the run searches harder (10-27 03:52) or asks the guessing brain. 10-27 05:37: in sql ':' has an action we know; on this line it has none yet."}
    dna -> defn: {class: readw}
  }
  n63: "63 · who" {
    class: col; grid-columns: 1; grid-gap: 14
    dna: "63 · who  DNA" {class: newborn; s108: "3 > - | - | 48 memory"; later1: "(later: who is, who are, who did, who was: a row each)"; tooltip: "s108 ends R1. 10-22 rev17: ’Who is, who are, who did and who was each fire a different row inside the one neuron for who, and each row points at a different formula: a different place to look, a different thing to read, a different thing to say.’ 10-24: ’Go get me some coffee now... me who, coffee what... involves 4 questions who, what, when, where that I have to ask myself when I hear or see those words’."}
    defn: "definition node of 63" {class: defn; def1: "wn: > return run (…a United Nations agency to coo…) [kept apart: the WHO agency]"; def2: "gcide: > return run (…Originally, an interrogative p…)"; def3: "mw: > return run (…what or which person or person…)"; def4: "mw: > return run (…the person or persons that : w…)"; rel1: "? > 139 (whom)  named beside who by gcide"; rel2: "? > 162 (whoever)  named by mw"; rel3: "? > 142 (person)  named by mw"; tooltip: "Rows from the real source lines: wn 1 (the WHO agency, kept apart by the check 'is this the data I asked for'), gcide 1, mw 2. The relationship rows: the symbols the definitions name beside who; the KIND of each relationship is not decided here (law 5: no facts from the builder's head); 10-23 P4 lists the kinds the compile builds from (kind of, part, has, plural, past, present, is, same spelling, derived from, used in...)."}
    m1: "memory node on 63.s108  (R1, the main run)" {class: mem; run: "1"; input: "61 3 62 3 63 (receipt)"; source: "user"; c_clock: "t0"; c_speaker: "user, heard"; c_q_know: "0"; c_q_remember: "0"; c_q_shape: "0"; c_path: "long"; c_gate_61: "61"; c_def_61: "> definition node of 61"; c_gate_62: "62"; c_def_62: "0: empty, search harder"; c_gate_63: "63"; c_def_63: "> definition node of 63"; c_say: "> what was said (R8)"; tooltip: "R1’s memory row, on the shard of who that ended it, with the whole data sheet inside (10-26 03:50: ’the data sheet just lives in a cell in the memory row’; his v4 run_sheet rows). Each cell was declared by the trigger that tripped and filled by a return. A cell still 0 after every return is in is the reason to search harder, never a silent end (10-27 03:52)."}
    m_more: "(another run ending at a different shard of who gets its own memory node here)" {class: note; tooltip: "His words 10-28: some neuron might have many memory tables because many of their dna shards end runs... each will have its own memory table and data runsheet in it."}
    dna -> defn: {class: readw}
    dna.s108 -> m1: {class: trig; label: "no remainder: 48 memory write run"}
  }
}
sources.src61.c52 -> after.n61.defn: {class: fill; label: "lays the rows: first memory"}
sources.src63.c52 -> after.n63.defn: {class: fill; label: "lays the rows: first memory"}
sources.src62.empty -> after.n62.defn: {class: not}
after.n61.defn -> after.n63.m1: {class: fill; label: "def.61"}
after.n63.defn -> after.n63.m1: {class: fill; label: "def.63"}
main.hall.he -> after.n63.dna: {class: readw; label: "the same neuron"}
inspect: "8 · INSPECTION RUN: a row that decides which row to hit (10-23 P2 B1; P3 B1). On this line no gate has two rows for one arrival, so it is not called." {
  class: lane
  grid-columns: 3
  i1: "the gate" {
    class: col; grid-columns: 1; grid-gap: 14
  gate_x: "a gate with two shards for one before" {class: neuron; sA: "x > y | 46 inspect | -"; sB: "x > z | 46 inspect | -"; tooltip: "When the hop’s read by before + remainder head cannot pick one shard, the row’s call column holds the inspection run. 10-23 P2 B1: ’that trigger says, Hey, I need to find the noun, or I need to find the verb and the noun, or I need to see if there’s any adjectives in this sentence, because that determines what row I actually have to hit inside my node. And so that trigger might look to the script and say, Oh, I have this and this and this, and then it might go back to that node and hit a different shard.’"}
  }
  i2: "the call" {
    class: col; grid-columns: 1; grid-gap: 14
  r46: "46 · read (the inspection run)" {class: neuron; s286: "x > x | - | -"; tooltip: "The inspection run: 1. reads the main run’s input row: the symbols after this gate and before it (10-20 00:31: ’triggers will later look at the run if we need them to look at other parts of the run’); 2. reads their definition nodes: class, tense, perspective; 3. reads the run’s sheet: speaker, what is known; 4. fills its own small sheet; its answer is the shard to hit; 5. returns to the gate, which hits THAT shard (10-23 P3 B1: ’run a DNA row that actually decides what DNA row that actually needs to get hit in that gate’). 10-23 P2 B2: ’it might be more efficient if the DNA row has that... so that the run table becomes more precise’ [open: an extra column on the row]."}
  sh: "inspection sheet" {class: sheet; c_after: "gates after"; c_before: "gates before"; c_classes: "from their definition nodes"; c_answer: "> the shard to hit"}
  }
  i1.gate_x.sA -> i2.r46: {class: call; label: "call"}
  i2.r46 -> i2.sh: {class: readw}
  i2.sh -> i1.gate_x.sB: {class: call; label: "re-hit that shard"}
}
ending: "9 · THE END OF R1 and the TIMELINE: no remainder > memory > timeline > stop (10-26 03:50)" {
  class: lane
  grid-columns: 3
  writes: "the two write runs" {
    class: col; grid-columns: 1; grid-gap: 14
  m48: "48 · memory (write run)" {class: neuron; s287: "63 > 49 | 44 copy | -"; tooltip: "A copy run the ending shard excites; the trigger writes nothing itself (10-26 03:59). It lays run_memory(1, receipt, user) on 63.s108 and the run_sheet rows inside it. 10-21 18:39: ’if no remainder write to memory node (temporary run data, triggered runs data, and results/outcome data)’."}
  t49: "49 · timeline (write run)" {class: neuron; s288: "48 > - | - | -"; tooltip: "’spawns its memory, which reaches the timeline by a trigger hop. Never a sweep’ (10-23 P3 B1). One row: the clock reading, the receipt pointer, a couple of key words (10-16 06:40). Then it stops: ’timeline is only a memory table has no dna table ahead of it’ (10-26 03:50)."}
  }
  timeline: "TIMELINE after this line" {class: tl; r1: "t0 | SQ1 > 57.s101 | 57"; r2: "t0 | SQ2 > 58.s102 | 58"; r3: "t0 | SQ3 > 59.s103 | 59"; r4: "t1 | R2 born 61 > 40.s250 | 41 61"; r5: "t2 | R3 sources 61: 12 return runs, compile | 51 61"; r6: "t3 | R4 born 62 > 40.s261 | 41 62"; r7: "t4 | R5 sources 62: 0 returns | 51 62"; r8: "t5 | R6 born 63 > 40.s268 | 41 63"; r9: "t6 | R7 sources 63: 4 return runs, compile | 51 63"; r10: "t7 | R1 define : who > 63.s108 | 61 63"; r11: "t8 | R8 say > 56.s | 56"; tooltip: "A row per run that ended, in the order the drawing says they happen. 't' is the clock neuron's reading. The pointer is the run's memory node; the key words are a couple of gates. The born runs of the symbols inside the definitions (the cascade) add their own rows, not listed."}
  writes.m48.s287 -> writes.t49.s288: {class: trig; label: "memory > timeline"}
  writes.t49.s288 -> timeline: {class: fill; label: "one row; then stops"}
}
after.n63.m1 -> ending.writes.m48: {class: readw; label: "the row it lays"}
output: "10 · OUTPUT: a separate mechanism from the same memory table (10-26 03:50): when no declared cell of R1 is empty" {
  class: lane
  grid-columns: 4
  o1: "from the memory table" {
    class: col; grid-columns: 1; grid-gap: 14
  when: "no declared cell of R1 empty?" {class: pick; tooltip: "10-26 03:50: 'output is triggered by a completely separate mech from the data sheet... output to timeline and output to user, or output to mother all come from the same memory table... such that when the run's data table is complete the run can populate and finish its larger formulas'. On this line def.62 stays 0: the search-harder run (lane 6) must answer first, or the brain says so in its own words."}
  formulas: "the larger formulas over the sheet" {class: note; tooltip: "10-23 P3 B2: substitute the cells as variables into the larger formulas that form the reasoning and the answer. On a fresh brain nothing is bound to define yet; the material is the sheet: the compiled definitions of 61 and 63 and the empty cell of 62."}
  balance: "makes sense: desired vs current vs new" {class: note; tooltip: "10-23 P5-9 B1: 'desired equation on one side, current equation which you know is not yet the desired, and your new equation... closer or further away... progressing or digressing'. Not balanced: step back to the decision that picked this path and take another row; the failed try stays in memory marked wrong (10-22 rev17)."}
  }
  o2: "say and out" {
    class: col; grid-columns: 1; grid-gap: 14
  s56: "56 · say" {class: neuron; s289: "48 > 60 | - | -"; tooltip: "The say run. 10-26 05:12: ’the only way we have found that a trigger is needed is with a say key’ [open: what the say key is]. The reply is a tree of arrays: the first word’s array, each pick opens the next (10-23 P3 B2; 10-19: ’build choose first word array... does that balance now... ship output’). 10-27 03:52: ’it chooses what it wants to say’. No sentence is drawn here on purpose: ’define? canis? find? is not it choosing what to say’ (10-27 04:07)."}
  dec: "DECODER: gates > symbols" {class: encoder; tooltip: "10-23 P3 B2: What comes out of the decoder? Just numbers. Just gate numbers... maybe head data: perspective, situation, topic."}
  to_user: "brain > user mailbox" {class: mail}
  to_sixth: "> sixth.db mailbox" {class: mail; tooltip: "10-25 msg 41: run output to sixth."}
  }
  o1.when -> o1.formulas -> o1.balance
  o1.balance -> o2.s56.s289: {label: "balanced"}
  o1.balance -> o1.formulas: {class: not; label: "digressing: another row"}
  o2.s56.s289 -> o2.dec -> o2.to_user
  o2.s56.s289 -> o2.to_sixth: {class: spawn}
}
ending.timeline -> output.o1.when: {class: trig; label: "the memory row exists"}
after.n63.m1 -> output.o2.s56: {class: fill; label: "say > cell say"}
others: "11 · THE SIXTH and THE GUESSING BRAIN" {
  class: lane
  grid-columns: 2
  sixth: "the sixth" {
    class: col; grid-columns: 1; grid-gap: 14
    see: "sixth.db reads the brain: the new memory row, the timeline, the newborns, the empty cell def.62" {class: brain; tooltip: "10-23 P2: 'the me that talks to me, that runs me... asks me questions or helps me reason or observes things that I'm doing and tries to find more efficient'. 10-23 03:18: 'in the sixth its own questions and prompts from observations basically (sight) of the brain are what is driving the questions and actions to happen... Why do I do that? hmm I should test if there a better way, or check to see if that is actually correct'."}
    ask: "it types its own line into its mailbox, speaker = sixth" {class: mail; tooltip: "What it asks is its own: 'wait why did I think that that way... or does this meaning actually make sense?' (10-23 01:28); 'why did the cell not populate' (10-27 03:52). 10-16 05:50: 'at first we would have to prompt these, but later many of these are written by the sixth'. 10-22 rev17: it reaches the main brain only through mother and mailboxes; whatever SQL it writes into the main brain is told, not learned."}
    r9: "a new run R9 through the same hallway; SQ2 now finds R1's memory" {class: sheet}
    see -> ask -> r9
  }
  guess: "the guessing brain" {
    class: col; grid-columns: 1; grid-gap: 14
    g_ask: "a cell no source fills (def.62) > the question goes to the guessing brain through mother" {class: mail; tooltip: "10-23 P5-9: memory first, then state, then sources, then guess."}
    g_g: "a guess of what the meaning should be: announced, never lost" {class: brain; tooltip: "10-23 P5-9: 'I don't know; I have a guess I believe is likely; would you like to test it?' 10-15 21:17: 'Always state that this is a guess or belief not known source truth'."}
    g_wrong: "the sixth says why it was wrong > back prop over the adjusted corpus > guess again until it balances" {class: brain; tooltip: "10-23 03:18."}
    g_ask -> g_g: {class: route}
    g_g -> g_wrong: {class: not}
  }
}
output.o2.to_sixth -> others.sixth.see: {class: route}
others.sixth.r9 -> line.r1: {class: hop; label: "a new line, speaker = sixth"}
sources.src62.empty -> others.guess.g_ask: {class: spawn; label: "search harder"}
others.guess.g_g -> after.n63.m1: {class: fill; label: "def.62 = a guess, marked"}
open: "12 · WHAT HIS WORDS LEAVE OPEN (his calls, not filled)" {
  class: lane
  grid-columns: 5
  o1: "1. born inline or retry" {class: open; tooltip: "10-20 01:22 'writes it, and triggers born... goes to space' (inline) vs 10-21 18:39 'traverse back to the run start and try run again' (retry). Both his."}
  o2: "2. the receipt: physics or write run" {class: open; tooltip: "Audit 10-25 row 5: whether the hop's physics writes the receipt row or a write run does."}
  o3: "3. the sheet's physical form" {class: open; tooltip: "10-26 03:50: rows pointed to by the memory row, a table per run, or one cell. His v4 brain.db holds run_sheet rows keyed by run; the drawing follows that."}
  o4: "4. the compile" {class: open; tooltip: "10-23 P4 B1: 'I don't know that the compile is correct yet'. Diff and similar as drawn is his sketch of it, not a formula."}
  o5: "5. sourcing depth of the cascade" {class: open; tooltip: "10-23 P4: 'everything expands so fast'. Every symbol born from a definition text is sourced in turn; how deep is his call."}
  o6: "6. the say key" {class: open; tooltip: "10-26 05:12: 'the only way we have found that a trigger is needed is with a say key'. Not found in his words before that line."}
  o7: "7. one formula neuron or many" {class: open; tooltip: "Drawn: one formula neuron (55) with a row per formula. 10-23 08:47 says 'a pointer to the shortcut neuron formula for that'. His call."}
  o8: "8. what counts as 'correct' the first time" {class: open; tooltip: "His words 10-28: 'the first time it works, and is correct'. Drawn as: ok + a read run finds the table. His call."}
  o9: "9. the standing questions in the core" {class: open; tooltip: "10-23 P3 B2 'maybe those three questions'. Drawn as core neurons 57, 58, 59 because a fresh brain has nothing else to ask with."}
  o10: "10. define in the core or not" {class: open; tooltip: "Drawn: not in the core; born on this line (10-27 05:37). If in the core, SQ3 says yes and the run jumps to its formula: 'look up definition memory print' (10-25 msg 51)."}
}
